A hospital wing is optimizing the allocation of patients requiring specific specialized equipment to compatible treatment rooms. The facility comprises five patients (indexed 0 to 4) and seven treatment rooms (indexed 0 to 6). Due to constraints imposed by equipment compatibility and room specifications, not every patient can be assigned to every treatment room. There are exactly 15 predefined feasible compatibility assignments, specified as follows:
- Patient 0 can be assigned to treatment room 0, treatment room 2, treatment room 4, or treatment room 5;
- Patient 1 can be assigned to treatment room 2 or treatment room 6;
- Patient 2 can be assigned to treatment room 0, treatment room 2, or treatment room 3;
- Patient 3 can be assigned to treatment room 0 or treatment room 4;
- Patient 4 can be assigned to treatment room 1, treatment room 2, treatment room 3, or treatment room 4.

The operational objective is to maximize the number of concurrent patient treatment sessions. Strict business rules mandate that, at any given time, each patient may be assigned to at most one treatment room, and each treatment room may receive at most one patient. Furthermore, all scheduling assignments must be confined exclusively to the aforementioned 15 feasible compatibility assignments.

Formulate an allocation scheme that maximizes the number of successfully matched patient–treatment room pairs while satisfying all stated constraints.

A cloud computing cluster is optimizing the assignment logic of its central task scheduler. The facility comprises seven incoming computational tasks (indexed 0 to 6) and five servers equipped with specific hardware accelerators (indexed 0 to 4). Due to constraints imposed by software dependencies and hardware compatibility, not every computational task can be executed on every server. There are exactly 15 predefined feasible compatibility paths, specified as follows:
- Incoming computational task 0 can connect to server 3 or server 4;
- Incoming computational task 1 can connect to server 0, server 3, or server 4;
- Incoming computational task 2 can connect to server 0 or server 3;
- Incoming computational task 3 can connect to server 1 or server 2;
- Incoming computational task 4 can connect to server 0 or server 1;
- Incoming computational task 5 can connect to server 2;
- Incoming computational task 6 can connect to server 0, server 3, or server 4.

The operational objective is to maximize the number of concurrent task execution assignments. Strict business rules mandate that, at any given time, each incoming computational task may be assigned to at most one server, and each server may receive at most one incoming computational task. Furthermore, all scheduling assignments must be confined exclusively to the aforementioned 15 feasible compatibility paths.

Formulate a scheduling scheme that maximizes the number of successfully matched task–server pairs while satisfying all stated constraints.

An e-commerce fulfillment center is optimizing its assignment system for orders containing specific hazardous materials. The facility processes six distinct orders containing hazardous materials (indexed 0 to 5) and operates five packing stations certified and equipped to handle those material classes (indexed 0 to 4). Due to strict safety regulations regarding certification compatibility between order material classes and packing station equipment, not every order can be routed to every station. There are exactly 11 predefined feasible connection paths where an order's material class matches a station's certification, specified as follows:
- Order 0 can be assigned to Packing Station 0, Packing Station 1, or Packing Station 4;
- Order 1 can be assigned to Packing Station 2;
- Order 2 can be assigned to Packing Station 0, Packing Station 1, or Packing Station 4;
- Order 3 can be assigned to Packing Station 0, Packing Station 1, or Packing Station 2;
- Order 4 can be assigned to Packing Station 3;
- Order 5 has no compatible packing stations based on current certifications.

The operational objective is to maximize the number of concurrent order fulfillment assignments. Strict business rules mandate that, at any given time, each order containing hazardous materials may be routed to at most one packing station, and each packing station may process at most one order. Furthermore, all scheduling assignments must be confined exclusively to the aforementioned 11 feasible connection paths defined by the certification compatibility.

Formulate an assignment scheme that maximizes the number of successfully matched order–station pairs while satisfying all stated constraints.

An automotive manufacturer operates a rigid automated assembly line dedicated to multi-model vehicle production. The facility has received an urgent order requiring the assembly of four distinct vehicle production orders, designated V0 through V3. To complete the assembly of these vehicles, all units must strictly adhere to an identical assembly sequence path, sequentially passing through three critical assembly workstations: W0, W1, and W2. Due to the facility's rigid automotive assembly line design, once the input sequence of vehicles at the first assembly workstation (W0) is determined, all subsequent workstations must strictly follow this same vehicle sequencing; reordering between workstations is not permitted.

Based on current process parameters, the assembly durations (in minutes) for each vehicle production order at the respective workstations are as follows:
- Vehicle V0: 47 minutes at W0, 11 minutes at W1, and 33 minutes at W2;
- Vehicle V1: 13 minutes at W0, 15 minutes at W1, and 48 minutes at W2;
- Vehicle V2: 47 minutes at W0, 12 minutes at W1, and 46 minutes at W2;
- Vehicle V3: 50 minutes at W0, 20 minutes at W1, and 49 minutes at W2.

The production scheduling problem is subject to strict physical constraints: at any given time, each assembly workstation can handle only one vehicle, and each vehicle can be processed at only one workstation simultaneously. Parallel processing or preemption is strictly prohibited. The plant manager's objective is to devise an optimal vehicle sequencing scheme that minimizes the total line completion time—defined as the time elapsed from the entry of the first vehicle into workstation W0 until the completion and exit of the last vehicle from workstation W2—thereby enabling the fastest possible order fulfillment.

A semiconductor fabrication facility has received an urgent order requiring the processing of four distinct wafer lots, designated W0 through W3. To complete the manufacturing of these wafers, all lots must strictly adhere to an identical process route, sequentially passing through three critical fabrication layers: L0, L1, and L2. Due to the facility's rigid automated flow line design, once the input sequence of wafer lots at the first layer processing station (L0) is determined, all subsequent stations must strictly follow this same processing sequence; reordering between stations is not permitted.

Based on current process parameters, the layer exposure and etching durations (in minutes) for each wafer lot at the respective fabrication layers are as follows:
- Wafer Lot W0: 17 minutes at L0, 45 minutes at L1, and 24 minutes at L2;
- Wafer Lot W1: 28 minutes at L0, 29 minutes at L1, and 35 minutes at L2;
- Wafer Lot W2: 16 minutes at L0, 20 minutes at L1, and 31 minutes at L2;
- Wafer Lot W3: 28 minutes at L0, 14 minutes at L1, and 31 minutes at L2.

The production scheduling problem is subject to strict physical constraints: at any given time, each layer processing station can handle only one wafer lot, and each wafer lot can be processed at only one station simultaneously. Parallel processing or preemption is strictly prohibited. The plant manager's objective is to devise an optimal wafer lot layer processing order that minimizes the total fab cycle time—defined as the time elapsed from the entry of the first wafer lot into station L0 until the completion and exit of the last wafer lot from station L2—thereby enabling the fastest possible order fulfillment.

A Food and Beverage Canning Facility has received an urgent order requiring the processing of four distinct product batches, designated B0 through B3. To complete the canning process, all batches must strictly adhere to an identical process route, sequentially passing through three critical canning line stages: S0, S1, and S2. Due to the facility's fixed-sequence canning line design, once the input sequence of batches at the first stage (S0) is determined, all subsequent stages must strictly follow this same processing sequence; reordering between stages is not permitted.

Based on current process parameters, the changeover and processing durations (in minutes) for each batch at the respective stages are as follows:
- Batch B0: 43, 10, and 48 minutes at stages S0, S1, and S2, respectively;
- Batch B1: 23, 33, and 16 minutes;
- Batch B2: 49, 43, and 48 minutes;
- Batch B3: 19, 38, and 20 minutes.

The production scheduling problem is subject to strict physical constraints: at any given time, each canning line stage can handle only one batch, and each batch can be processed at only one stage simultaneously. Parallel processing or preemption is strictly prohibited. The facility manager's objective is to devise an optimal product batch sequencing scheme that minimizes the total line changeover and production time—defined as the time elapsed from the entry of the first batch into stage S0 until the completion and exit of the last batch from stage S2—thereby enabling the fastest possible order fulfillment.
